<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later -->
<device schemaVersion="1.3" xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xs:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="CMSIS-SVD.xsd">
  <name>ESP32C6_RADIO</name>
  <version>0.1.0</version>
  <description>Undocumented ESP32-C6 radio registers as esp-sdr-zephyr knows them, additions to Espressif's SVD (github.com/espressif/svd at be20aa1): doc/registers/svdmerge.py merges them, doc/registers/svd2md.py renders doc/undocumented-registers-esp32c6.md. Peripherals in group ANALOG_I2C are analog I2C blocks, not memory-mapped: base address block &lt;&lt; 8, one 8-bit register per offset.</description>
  <addressUnitBits>8</addressUnitBits>
  <width>32</width>
  <size>32</size>
  <access>read-write</access>
  <peripherals>
    <peripheral>
      <name>DUMP</name>
      <description>
        Dump and DAC engine
        Same RUN, TRIGGER, DONE and COUNT bits as the S3 at another address (*upstream*
        `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/families/c5_c6_c61/receiver.c`,
        `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h`). Receive runs at 80 MS/s
        only; the transmit side is a DAC engine at 0x600a900c (DUMP_DAC_TRIG_REG,
        below), which no vendor code programs: the C6 `dactrig()` only writes a ramp
        into 0x40840000 (*decompile* C6 `librftest.a` `mac_common.o`). Base 0x600a9000
        has no name in the HAL. Each capture and each playback needs the clocks in
        MODEM_SYSCON forced on and an SRAM block handed to the engine in
        HP_SYSTEM_SRAM_USAGE_CONF_REG.

        **Receive rate.** The C6 `adctrig()` starts only mode 0 by software; the other
        modes wait for a hardware event. It writes no part of DUMP_MODE_REG (0x600a9008)
        but the source field, DUMP_SRC, and never reads the argument that the C5 and C61
        versions turn into a rate (*decompile* C6 `librftest.a` `mac_common.o`):

        | `adctrig()` mode | DUMP_SRC | Also |
        |---|---|---|
        | 0 | 15 | software start |
        | 1, 3 | 1 | |
        | 2 | 0 | |
        | 4 | 2 | |
        | 5 | 3 | tries to send a frame each poll, at most one per `tx_frame_delay_us` (800 by default) |
        | 6 | 4 | as mode 5 |
        | 7 | 5 | Wi-Fi MAC event selector WIFIMAC_EVENT_SEL_REG (0x600a4e30): bit 8 set, bits 7:0 the event |
        | 8 | 0 | BTBB_DUMP_GATE (0x600a20b4 bit 0) set, lanes 0x0e to 0x11, BLE receiver started |

        Upstream found no other source that gives a uniform stream, and neither
        `adc_rate_set()` nor candidate divider bits changed the timing (*upstream*
        `git show 34bb79b:docs/rx-controls.md`, "C6 sample-rate investigation").
        Upstream's C6 ring is gapless at 80 MS/s, rotating SRAM blocks 1 and 2 under a
        circular DUMP_CTRL_REG (DUMP_CIRCULAR, bit 17, count 16384), and consumes it as FFT
        windows (*upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/ring_capture.c:96-131,
        1485-1507`): the 160 MHz CPU has 2 cycles per sample, too few to decimate the
        whole stream.

        Leads for a continuous C6 receive below 80 MS/s, none tried:

        - The modem diagnostic bus into PARL_IO. A third-party project, not re-checked
          here, reports for the ESP32-C5 that the dump's I/Q MSBs appear on MODEM_DIAG
          (Q[9:6] on lines 9:6, I[9:6] on 19:16), and that routed through the GPIO
          matrix into PARL_IO, a cyclic 40 MS/s capture takes every second sample of
          the 80 MS/s bus as 4+4-bit I/Q, bit-perfect in a bounded capture (it claims
          no gapless running across ring wraps;
          [Twotoz/C5VRX](https://github.com/Twotoz/C5VRX/blob/a165e19db47ebd49051217d7c968a1b8220c20fb/docs/continuous-iq-findings.md)).
          The C6 selector registers are DUMP_DIAG_SEL0_REG and DUMP_DIAG_SEL1_REG
          (0x600a9018 and 0x600a901c), and the diag lines are enabled in
          IO_MUX_MODEM_DIAG_EN_REG (0x600900bc); both below. Which selector
          value puts receive I/Q on the C6 bus is unknown. Sampling below 80 MHz
          decimates without filtering, so the analog low-pass has to be narrowed first.
        - The 802.11p clock mode: `phy_11p_set(1, m)` writes MODEM_SYSCON_WIFI_BB_CFG_REG
          (0x600a981c) bits 4:2 = 4 for m = 0, else 5, presumably for 10 and 5 MHz
          channels, and clears AGC_R30_REG (0x600a7030) bit 5 (*decompile* C6 `libphy.a`
          `phy_feature.o`). Whether the dump follows the slower clock is unknown.
        - The BLE dump (mode 8 above): rate unknown; it runs only while the BLE receiver
          does.
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600a9000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x4</offset>
        <size>0x1c</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>CTRL</name>
          <description>
            Receive side: run, trigger and count of a capture
            No rate field, unlike the S3's: receive runs at 80 MS/s only.
            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_priv.h:77-81`,
            `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:302-310`; *upstream*
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h:43-46` (DUMP_RUN, DUMP_TRIGGER,
            DUMP_DONE, DUMP_COUNT)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RUN</name>
              <description>
                Enables the engine, as on the S3.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TRIGGER</name>
              <description>
                As on the S3, write 1, then 0, to start a one-shot capture.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DONE</name>
              <description>
                As on the S3, reads 1 once DUMP_COUNT words are written. Write DUMP_COUNT
                with DUMP_DONE first to clear a stale DUMP_DONE, as the stock sequence does.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CIRCULAR</name>
              <description>
                Circular ring, in upstream's C6 ring code. Not used here.
                Evidence: *upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/ring_capture.c:104,
                1499-1505`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>COUNT</name>
              <description>
                Words to capture, 256 to 16380; 16380 samples take about 205 us.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[13:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>MODE</name>
          <description>
            Dump source; the live write index in upstream's ring
            Upstream's ring code reads the live write index from the low bits of this
            register.
            Evidence: *upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/ring_capture.c:96, 1507`
            (write index)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B23_21</name>
              <description>
                Not a rate field, unlike on the C5 and C61, where these bits take a rate
                code: written bits do not stay, and captures and replays keep their timing.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11; C5 and C61: *upstream*
                `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c5/chip.h:11`,
                `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c61/chip.h:20-21`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[23:21]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRC</name>
              <description>
                Dump source; 15 = raw receive I/Q, as the stock `adctrig()` (see "Receive
                rate" above for the other sources). The library writes 15.
                Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_priv.h:82-84`; *upstream*
                `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h:6, 39`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[18:15]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DAC_TRIG</name>
          <description>
            Transmit side: run, loop, rate and count of a playback
            8 bytes above DUMP_CTRL_REG, as on the S3. Bits 14, 16 and 17 do not read back.

            - One word per sample, I in bits 9:0 and Q in 19:10, as for the S3's DAC (see
              "Sample word formats"): a complex tone of k cycles in n words plays at +k/n
              of the rate (200 cycles in 16000 words: 0.5 MHz at 40 MS/s, 1 MHz at
              80 MS/s), its image 37 to 40 dB down. All-zero words play nothing, a
              constant word a carrier.
            - What playback needs: the dump clocks forced on as for a capture (without
              them DUMP_DAC_DONE still sets on time but nothing reaches the antenna; see
              MODEM_SYSCON_CLK_CONF_REG), SRAM block 2 handed to the engine
              (HP_SYSTEM_SRAM_USAGE = 4, that is 0x60095004 bits 11:8, samples from
              0x40840000), and the transmit chain on without its tone: the vendor CW
              (`esp_phy_wifi_tx_tone(1, 6, 80)`), then bit 18 (FE_TONE_EN) of 0x600a0420
              and 0x600a0424 cleared. That leaves the DAC source select, FE_DAC_SRC
              (0x600a0414 bits 1:0), at 0, the tone generator's setting (see
              "Front end").
            - While the engine owns the block, CPU stores are dropped and loads return
              other data, as during a capture.

            No vendor blob or ROM of the C6, C5 or C61 writes this register. Only upstream
            code does: the C5 transmitter of its first upload (`emit_dma()`, removed in
            ab76232), and the C61 capture and ring code, which writes 0 to it before each
            capture. Upstream's S31 code clears bit 31 of its counterpart, 0x2010900c, when
            it sets up the dump.
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11 (read-back, sample format, what playback
            needs, CPU access)
            Evidence: *decompile* (no vendor or ROM writer)
            Evidence: `git show e605684:main/c5_main.c`, `emit_dma()` (C5 transmitter)
            Evidence: *upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c61/chip.h:12, 17`,
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/ring_capture.c:1493` (C61)
            Evidence: *upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32s31/burst/receiver.c:37,
            123` (S31)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xc</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAC_RUN</name>
              <description>
                Written with DUMP_DAC_COUNT and with DUMP_DAC_LOOP clear: one pass, with no
                trigger pulse as on the S3.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11
              </description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>B27_20</name>
              <description>
                Read back as written; effect untested. The S3 `dactrig()` writes the count's
                low byte here (see the ESP32-S3 page); upstream's C5 replay uses it as a
                repeat count, 0 meaning endless with bit 19 set.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11 (read-back only); *upstream*
                `git show e605684:main/c5_main.c`, comment above `emit_dma()`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[27:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC_LOOP</name>
              <description>
                Set with DUMP_DAC_RUN (DUMP_B27_20 at 0): the engine replays the first
                DUMP_DAC_COUNT words back to back until DUMP_DAC_RUN is cleared;
                DUMP_DAC_DONE stays clear.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11
              </description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC_DONE</name>
              <description>
                Sets after one pass.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11
              </description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC_RATE_80M</name>
              <description>
                1 = 80 MS/s, 0 = 40 MS/s: a pass takes 4 cycles of the 160 MHz CPU per word
                at 40 MS/s (DUMP_DAC_COUNT 1000 to 16000), 2 at 80 MS/s (DUMP_DAC_COUNT
                16000). The rate is this bit, as on the S3, not bit 17 as in upstream's C5
                replay.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11
              </description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC_COUNT</name>
              <description>
                Words to play from the start of the owned block, up to 16383.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-11
              </description>
              <bitRange>[13:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CONFIG</name>
          <description>
            Lane selectors: the sources packed into a dump word
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LANES</name>
              <description>
                Lane selectors as the S3's DUMP_CONFIG_REG (0x60033d90, see the ESP32-S3
                page): four 6-bit lane selectors, lane n in bits 6n+5:6n. The library
                clears the field, then sets it to 0x000c2040. `adctrig()` mode 8, the BLE
                dump, uses lanes 0x0e to 0x11 (see "Receive rate" above).
                Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:258`; *upstream*
                `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h:38`
                Evidence: *decompile* C6 `librftest.a` `mac_common.o` (mode 8)
              </description>
              <bitRange>[24:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>0,1</dimIndex>
          <name>DIAG_SEL%s</name>
          <description>
            Modem diagnostic bus selectors
            `gpio_diag_init_ble()` writes 1 to DUMP_DIAG_SEL0_REG and
            `coex_hw_debug_matrix_config()` 2 to DUMP_DIAG_SEL1_REG, on the C6 as on the
            C5, which has the pair at 0x600a9404 and 0x600a9408. On the C6 each function
            then enables diag lines in IO_MUX_MODEM_DIAG_EN_REG: 0xffff0000 and
            0x0000000f. Which selector value puts receive I/Q on the C6 bus is unknown
            (see the leads above).
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 and C5 `libble_app.a` `gpio_diag.c.o`, `libcoexist.a`
            `coexist_hw.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>HP_SYS</name>
      <version>espressif/svd be20aa1</version>
      <description>
        SRAM bank ownership
      </description>
      <prependToName>HP_SYSTEM_</prependToName>
      <baseAddress>0x60095000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x4</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>SRAM_USAGE_CONF</name>
          <description>
            Hands an SRAM block to the dump engine
            Other bits: documented 0
            The library saves the register and restores it after each use. Undocumented
            behaviour:

            - While the engine owns a block the CPUs cannot reach any of it, so nothing
              else may live there; the linker snippet reserves the block and lowers
              `_heap_sentry`. The engine owns the whole 128 KiB block during a capture,
              although a capture uses at most 64 KiB, so the library's other buffers live
              in block 3.
            - On the RISC-V core the switch must land before the engine starts: a
              `fence rw, rw` and a read-back follow each write.
            - Only block 2 can be the engine's. Block 3 ends in the ROM's .bss and .data
              (0x4087e610 to 0x40880000), which ROM functions use at any time, and a
              Zephyr image with Wi-Fi fills blocks 0 and 1 (the C6 build of `apps/rfprobe`
              has `_end` at 0x408366b0 and its libc heap above that up to block 2), so
              upstream's rotation over blocks 1 and 2 does not fit.

            Evidence: `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:246-278`, `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:123-133`
            (save and restore)
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-08, `lib/esp_sdr/esp_sdr_bank_c6.ld:18-20`
            (whole block)
            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:262-265, 274-277` (fence)
            Evidence: *measured* build 2026-10-11 (only block 2);
            `modules/zephyr/soc/espressif/esp32c6/memory.h:29-40` (ROM .bss and .data);
            `lib/esp_sdr/esp_sdr_bank_c6.ld:29-30` (`_heap_sentry`, rfprobe heap)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MAC_DUMP_ALLOC</name>
              <description>
                Adds a 64 KiB offset (HAL). The library leaves it 0; upstream's ring clears
                it.
                Evidence: `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/register/soc/hp_system_reg.h:63-68`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRAM_USAGE</name>
              <description>
                One-hot: field bit n (register bit 8 + n) hands block n, 0x40800000 + n x
                128 KiB, to the engine, for capture and playback alike; 0 gives every block
                back to the CPUs. n = 2 (0x40840000, the stock `adctrig()` bank) is the
                capture bank. The HAL says "0: cpu use hp-memory, 1: mac-dump accessing
                hp-memory".
                Evidence: `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/register/soc/hp_system_reg.h:46-62`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>MODEM_SYSCON</name>
      <version>espressif/svd be20aa1</version>
      <description>
        Modem clocks and reset
        Documented registers; the entries give the behaviour the HAL does not document.
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600a9800</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x4</offset>
        <size>0x1c</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>CLK_CONF</name>
          <description>
            Modem clock enables, forced on for the dump engine
            Other bits: documented 30:22
            The dump engine needs this register, MODEM_SYSCON_CLK_CONF1_REG and
            MODEM_SYSCON_CLK_CONF_POWER_ST_REG forced on before each capture and each
            playback, as the vendor's `rftest_init()`/`phy_set_clk_conf()` does. The
            library writes 0xffffffff here, then 0x7ffff to MODEM_SYSCON_CLK_CONF1_REG,
            then 0xffffffff to MODEM_SYSCON_CLK_CONF_POWER_ST_REG. What playback needs
            besides: see DUMP_DAC_TRIG_REG.
            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:255-257`; *measured* bench
            2026-10-11 (playback); *upstream*
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h:35-36`;
            `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/include/modem/modem_syscon_reg.h:21-27, 82-87`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CLK_DATA_DUMP_EN</name>
              <description>
                Written 1 with the rest of the register (0xffffffff).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLK_DATA_DUMP_MUX</name>
              <description>
                Written 1 with the rest of the register (0xffffffff).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CLK_CONF_POWER_ST</name>
          <description>
            Forced to 0xffffffff for the dump engine
            Other bits: documented 31:8
            Written 0xffffffff before each capture and each playback, the last of the three
            clock writes (see MODEM_SYSCON_CLK_CONF_REG).
            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:255-257`; *measured* bench
            2026-10-11 (playback); *upstream*
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h:35-36`;
            `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/include/modem/modem_syscon_reg.h:151`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xc</addressOffset>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>MODEM_RST_CONF</name>
          <description>
            Modem resets, pulsed at boot against stale modem state
            Other bits: documented 31:29, 27:24, 22, 18:14, 10, 8
            Undocumented: the modem subsystem keeps the previous firmware's state across a
            chip reset (USB-Serial-JTAG, esptool). The library writes 0xffffffff, then 0,
            here and 0xf, then 0, to MODEM_LPCON_RST_CONF_REG at `PRE_KERNEL_1`, before the
            Wi-Fi driver starts, through `modem_syscon_ll_reset_all()` and
            `modem_lpcon_ll_reset_all()`, as ESP-IDF's
            `esp_system_reset_modules_on_exit()` does on a restart.
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09 (symptoms under "Stale modem
            state"); *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:29-48`;
            `modules/hal/espressif/components/hal/esp32c6/include/hal/modem_syscon_ll.h:325-329`,
            `modules/hal/espressif/components/esp_system/port/soc/esp32c6/system_internal.c:45-46`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CLK_CONF1</name>
          <description>
            Bits 18:0 forced on for the dump engine
            Other bits: documented 23:0
            Bits 18:0 written 1 (0x7ffff) before each capture and each playback, the second
            of the three clock writes (see MODEM_SYSCON_CLK_CONF_REG).
            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:255-257`; *measured* bench
            2026-10-11 (playback); *upstream*
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h:35-36`;
            `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/include/modem/modem_syscon_reg.h:281`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WIFI_BB_CFG</name>
          <description>
            Wi-Fi baseband configuration; the 802.11p clock mode in bits 4:2
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WIFI_BB_CFG</name>
              <description>
                Bits 4:2: `phy_11p_set(1, m)` writes 4 for m = 0, else 5, presumably for 10
                and 5 MHz channels, and clears AGC_R30_REG bit 5. Whether the dump follows
                the slower clock is unknown (a lead under "Dump and DAC engine").
                Evidence: *decompile* C6 `libphy.a` `phy_feature.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>MODEM_LPCON</name>
      <version>espressif/svd be20aa1</version>
      <description>
        Modem LP reset
        Documented register; the behaviour the HAL does not document is under
        MODEM_SYSCON_MODEM_RST_CONF_REG.
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600af000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x24</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>RST_CONF</name>
          <description>
            Modem LP resets, pulsed at boot against stale modem state
            The library writes 0xf, then 0, at `PRE_KERNEL_1` through
            `modem_lpcon_ll_reset_all()`, with MODEM_SYSCON_MODEM_RST_CONF_REG (see there
            for why).
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09 (symptoms under "Stale modem
            state"); *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:29-48`;
            `modules/hal/espressif/components/hal/esp32c6/include/hal/modem_lpcon_ll.h:266-270`,
            `modules/hal/espressif/components/esp_system/port/soc/esp32c6/system_internal.c:45-46`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RST_LP_TIMER</name>
              <description>
                Set, then cleared, at boot (0xf, then 0).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RST_I2C_MST</name>
              <description>
                Set, then cleared, at boot (0xf, then 0).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RST_COEX</name>
              <description>
                Set, then cleared, at boot (0xf, then 0).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RST_WIFIPWR</name>
              <description>
                Set, then cleared, at boot (0xf, then 0).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>IO_MUX</name>
      <version>espressif/svd be20aa1</version>
      <description>
        Modem diagnostic lines
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x60090000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xbc</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>MODEM_DIAG_EN</name>
          <description>
            Routes modem diagnostic lines into the GPIO matrix
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xbc</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MODEM_DIAG_EN</name>
              <description>
                Bit i routes modem_diag[i] into the GPIO matrix. After writing their
                selectors (DUMP_DIAG_SEL0_REG, DUMP_DIAG_SEL1_REG), `gpio_diag_init_ble()`
                enables 0xffff0000 here and `coex_hw_debug_matrix_config()` 0x0000000f. A
                lead for a continuous receive below 80 MS/s (see "Dump and DAC engine").
                Evidence: `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/register/soc/io_mux_reg.h:325-336`;
                *decompile* C6 `libble_app.a` `gpio_diag.c.o`, `libcoexist.a` `coexist_hw.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BTBB</name>
      <description>
        BTBB page (0x600a2000)
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600a2000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xb4</offset>
        <size>0x7f0</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>RB4</name>
          <description>
            Dump gate in bit 0
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xb4</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DUMP_GATE</name>
              <description>
                Cleared before every capture, as the stock sequence. The C6 `adctrig()`
                clears it in every mode but 8, the BLE baseband dump, which sets it (see
                "Receive rate" under "Dump and DAC engine"); upstream's ESP32-H2 notes call
                it the trigger source.
                Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:261`; *decompile* C6
                `librftest.a` `mac_common.o`; *upstream*
                `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32c6/chip.h:40`,
                `modules/lib/esp-sdr/docs/esp32h2.md:20`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R8A0</name>
          <description>
            Unknown; bits 31:30 written by the BT branch of `force_txon_mode()`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x8a0</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B31_30</name>
              <description>
                Only with the BT flag (its second argument), `force_txon_mode(on, bt, idx)`
                stops the hardware frequency engine (FE_HW_FREQ_STOP, 0x600a00c0 bit 26),
                writes 0x600af4b4 bit 0, FE_BT_FORCE (0x600a0910 bits 13:12) and these bits
                (all ones with `on`, 0 without), and restarts the engine if it ran before
                (see "Front end").
              </description>
              <bitRange>[31:30]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>WIFIMAC</name>
      <description>
        Wi-Fi MAC page (0x600a4000)
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600a4000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xe30</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>EVENT_SEL</name>
          <description>
            Wi-Fi MAC event selector, used by the dump's `adctrig()` mode 7
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 `librftest.a` `mac_common.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xe30</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B8</name>
              <description>
                Set by `adctrig()` mode 7.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EVENT</name>
              <description>
                The event, written by `adctrig()` mode 7 (DUMP_SRC 5; see "Receive rate"
                under "Dump and DAC engine").
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>AGC</name>
      <description>
        AGC force
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600a7000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x2c</offset>
        <size>0xb4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>FORCE</name>
          <description>
            Forced receive gain index, force bit and largest calibrated index
            Same layout as the S3's 0x6001c02c; upstream calls the register AGCPWR_CTRL7.
            On the C6, `force_rx_gain(force, index, 0)` writes only this register. A new
            forced index alone can leave stale receive state, so a gain change reruns the
            front-end setup.
            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_priv.h:110-112`; *decompile* C6
            `librftest.a` `phy_test.o` (`force_rx_gain`); *upstream*
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/burst_gain.h:11-12, 36`
            Evidence: `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:164-166` (front-end rerun)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x2c</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FORCE_INDEX</name>
              <description>
                Forced receive gain index.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FORCE_EN</name>
              <description>
                Force: 1 holds the index, 0 hands the gain to the AGC.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN_MAX</name>
              <description>
                Largest calibrated gain index, as on the S3: below 80 (library cap 79).
                Larger reads point at uncalibrated slots; the library treats them as 0.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[14:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R30</name>
          <description>
            Unknown; bit 5 cleared in the 802.11p clock mode
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B5</name>
              <description>
                `phy_11p_set(1, m)` writes MODEM_SYSCON_WIFI_BB_CFG_REG bits 4:2 and clears
                this bit (the 802.11p clock mode, a lead under "Dump and DAC engine").
                Evidence: *decompile* C6 `libphy.a` `phy_feature.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RB8</name>
          <description>
            Unknown; bits 2:0 written by `set_dump_mode()`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xb8</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B2_0</name>
              <description>
                `set_dump_mode(x)` clears FE_R958_REG (0x600a0958) bits 1:0 and writes 1
                here when x is 0, else 0.
                Evidence: C6 `librftest.a` `phy_test.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[2:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RDC</name>
          <description>
            Unknown; bit 16 cleared by `force_txon()`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xdc</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B16</name>
              <description>
                `force_txon(on)` clears it and sets FE_RX_FORCE (0x600a0910 bits 9:8) to 2,
                then FE_TX_FORCE (bits 11:10) to 3; off goes in reverse. `force_txon_mode()`
                calls `force_txon(0)`, then `force_txon(on)` (see "Front end").
              </description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>FE</name>
      <description>
        Front end
        Unused by the library (it has no C6 transmit path). Evidence marked *decompile* in this
        section comes from C6 `libphy.a` `phy_reg.o`, `phy_pbus.o`, `phy_init.o` and
        `phy_tx_cal.o`, `librftest.a` `rf_test.o`, `esp_phy_api.o`, `phy_test.o` and `wifi.o`,
        and the C6 ROM.

        How the vendor functions drive the front end:

        - `esp_phy_wifi_tx_tone(start, chan, backoff)` (`wifiscwout()`) tunes with
          `chip_v7_set_chan(chan, 0)` (above 2000 the argument is MHz, truncated to a channel:
          2437 to 2441 all give channel 6), forces the analog TX chain on through pbus debug
          mode (`ram_txcal_debuge_mode()`: `pbus_force_mode(1)`, `ram_pbus_xpd_tx_on()`, the
          calibrated DC offset, the TX clock), and starts tone 0 at frequency 0, attenuated by
          the calibrated value + backoff + 12 in quarter dB. It never calls `force_txon()`.
          Stopping runs ROM `txcal_work_mode()` (0x400047d6): tone off, TX clock off, pbus back
          to receive and to normal mode. On the bench the carrier arrives at the channel centre
          (*measured* bench 2026-10-11).
        - `ram_stop_tx_tone()` stops the tones and selects the data path, leaving the pbus
          forcing and the TX clock on: the C6 counterpart of the C5's `phy_stop_tx_tone(1)`.
          The DAC engine played with the source left at 0 (see "Dump and DAC engine"); with 3
          it is untested.
        - `force_txon(on)` clears `0x600a70dc` bit 16 and sets FE_RX_FORCE to 2, then
          FE_TX_FORCE to 3; off goes in reverse.
        - In `force_txon_mode(on, bt, idx)` the second argument, which the ESP32-S3 page calls
          mode, acts as a BT flag: the function always writes the power index force
          (FE_PWR_INDEX_FORCE = `on`, index in FE_PWR_INDEX), then calls `force_txon(0)` and
          `force_txon(on)`; only with the flag it first adds 32 to the index, stops the
          hardware frequency engine (FE_HW_FREQ_STOP), writes `0x600af4b4` bit 0, FE_BT_FORCE
          and `0x600a28a0` bits 31:30 (all ones with `on`, 0 without), and restarts the engine
          if it ran before.
        - `set_dump_mode(x)` clears FE_R958_REG bits 1:0 and writes `0x600a70b8` bits 2:0 = 1
          when x is 0, else 0 (C6 `librftest.a` `phy_test.o`).
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600a0000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xc0</offset>
        <size>0x89c</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>RC0</name>
          <description>
            Hardware frequency engine stop
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0xc0</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>HW_FREQ_STOP</name>
              <description>
                The hardware frequency engine stop: the BT branch of `force_txon_mode()` stops
                the engine (this bit) and restarts it afterwards if it ran before.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>PWR_INDEX</name>
          <description>
            Power index force
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x410</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PWR_INDEX_FORCE</name>
              <description>
                Forces the power index FE_PWR_INDEX. `force_txon_mode()` sets it to its `on`
                argument, so `force_txon_mode(0, ...)` releases the force.
                Evidence: *decompile* C6 `libphy.a` `phy_reg.o` (`force_txon_mode()` passes
                `on != 0`), C6 ROM `phy_force_pwr_index` (0x400057e2, bit 23 = its first argument)
              </description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PWR_INDEX</name>
              <description>
                The forced power index: `force_txon_mode(on, bt, idx)` writes idx here, plus 32
                with the BT flag.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[21:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DAC_SRC</name>
          <description>
            DAC source select
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x414</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAC_SRC</name>
              <description>
                DAC source select as the vendor uses it: `ram_start_tx_tone_step()` writes 0
                with a tone and 3 without, `ram_stop_tx_tone()` writes 3; the ROM tone
                functions leave it alone. The DAC engine plays with 0 (see "Dump and DAC
                engine"); with 3 it is untested.
                Evidence: *decompile*; *measured* bench 2026-10-11
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TXDC_CAL</name>
          <description>
            TX DC calibration comparator
            Layout as libphy `txdc_cal_new()` drives it. The result goes into pbus rows 2 and 3
            through `rom_pbus_force_test()`. The ROM `txdc_cal()` (0x4000761e) uses another
            layout on the same register and no C6 library calls it. Shell writes sometimes made
            a carrier, not reproducibly.
            Evidence: *decompile* `libphy.a` `phy_tx_cal.o`, C6 ROM; *measured* bench 2026-10-11
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x418</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXDC_CMP_ROW2</name>
              <description>
                Comparator output for pbus row 2 (presumably I).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDC_CMP_ROW3</name>
              <description>
                Comparator output for pbus row 3 (presumably Q).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDC_CAL_DONE</name>
              <description>
                Calibration done.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDC_CAL_EN</name>
              <description>
                Calibration enable.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDC_CAL_START</name>
              <description>
                Calibration start.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>0,1</dimIndex>
          <name>TONE%s</name>
          <description>
            Tones 0 and 1: enable, attenuation, frequency
            The vendor keeps bits 31:28 and writes 0 to bits 27:19. The vendor CW leaves tone 0
            at 0x00068400 or 0x00068c00: frequency 0, a carrier at the LO.
            Evidence: *decompile*; *measured* bench 2026-10-11
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x420</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TONE_EN</name>
              <description>
                Tone enable.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TONE_ATTEN</name>
              <description>
                The attenuation, negated.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[17:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TONE_FREQ</name>
              <description>
                The frequency >> 2. libphy `ram_start_tx_tone_step()` puts the frequency's low
                two bits in FE_TONE_FREQ_LO_REG; the ROM version splits the frequency so only
                while FE_TONE0_REG bit 29 is set, else it writes it unshifted and leaves
                FE_TONE_FREQ_LO_REG alone.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TONE_FREQ_LO</name>
          <description>
            Fine tone frequency: the low two bits of each tone's frequency
            libphy `ram_start_tx_tone_step()` puts the frequency's low two bits here. The ROM
            version does so only while FE_TONE0_REG bit 29 is set, else it writes the frequency
            unshifted and leaves this register alone.
            Evidence: *decompile*
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x42c</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TONE1_FREQ_LO</name>
              <description>
                Low two bits of tone 1's frequency.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TONE0_FREQ_LO</name>
              <description>
                Low two bits of tone 0's frequency.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TXDC</name>
          <description>
            TX DC value
            The C6 `set_txdc_dig(i, q)`, the only code that writes the register, writes only
            FE_TXDC_I and FE_TXDC_Q; the identical C61 function writes 0x600a0c08, the register
            upstream's first C5 code drove CPU-paced I/Q through (`TXDC_REG`, `emit_iq()`).
            Untested on the C6.
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 and C61 `librftest.a` `phy_test.o`; *upstream*
            `git show e605684:main/c5_main.c:32, 211-238`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x444</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXDC_Q</name>
              <description>
                TX DC value, Q.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[19:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDC_I</name>
              <description>
                TX DC value, I.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R44C</name>
          <description>
            Unknown; init sets bits 27 and 25 and clears bit 22
            ROM `fe_reg_init()` and libphy `reg_set_new()` set bits 27 and 25 and clear bit 22
            at init, at the step where the C61's `phy_fe_reg_init()` sets bits 25 (IQ swap) and
            26 (inject) of its TX DC register 0x600a0c08. That 25 and 27 are the C6's IQ swap
            and inject bits is a guess.
            Evidence: *decompile* (the writes; C61 `libphy.a` `phy_reg.o`); *speculative* (the
            meaning)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x44c</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B27</name>
              <description>
                Set at init.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>B25</name>
              <description>
                Set at init.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>B22</name>
              <description>
                Cleared at init.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>FORCE</name>
          <description>
            Receive, transmit and BT mode force pairs; receive and transmit clocks
            FE_RX_FORCE, FE_TX_FORCE and FE_BT_FORCE are force pairs, high bit force, low bit
            value: 0 released, 2 forced off, 3 forced on (inferred from all writers).
            Evidence: *decompile*
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x910</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TX_CLK</name>
              <description>
                Transmit clock, 3 on, 0 off, as ROM `set_txclk_en()` (0x40005268) writes it.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[17:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RX_CLK</name>
              <description>
                Receive clock, 3 on, 0 off, as ROM `set_rxclk_en()` (0x4000528c) writes it.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[15:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BT_FORCE</name>
              <description>
                BT mode force pair: 3 from the BT branch of `force_txon_mode()`,
                `txon_set(1)` and `bt_rx_force()`.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[13:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TX_FORCE</name>
              <description>
                Transmit force pair: 3 from `force_txon(1)` and `txon_set(1)`; 2 from
                `force_txrx_off(1)` and ROM `phy_close_pa(1)`.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[11:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RX_FORCE</name>
              <description>
                Receive force pair: 2 from `force_txon(1)`, `txon_set(1)` and
                `force_txrx_off(1)`; 3 from `bt_rx_force()` and ROM `fe_adc_on()`.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[9:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R958</name>
          <description>
            Unknown; `set_dump_mode()` clears bits 1:0
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x958</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B1_0</name>
              <description>
                Cleared by `set_dump_mode(x)`.
                Evidence: C6 `librftest.a` `phy_test.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>I2C_ANA_MST</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C master
        `I2C_ANA_MST` at 0x600af800 is in the HAL; the register names are the HAL's.
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x600af800</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>0x24</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>0,1</dimIndex>
          <name>I2C%s_CTRL</name>
          <description>
            Command word of master 0 and master 1
            The ROM uses the same command word as the ESP32-S3: one command word per access,
            then poll I2C_ANA_MST_BUSY; block, register and I2C_ANA_MST_B26 for a read
            (0x04000000 base), plus data and I2C_ANA_MST_WRITE for a write (0x05000000 base).
            The result is in I2C_ANA_MST_DATA.
            Evidence: `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/include/modem/i2c_ana_mst_reg.h:15-45`;
            *decompile* C6 ROM 0x40003d88
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B26</name>
              <description>
                Unknown. The ROM sets it in every command word, as on the ESP32-S3; the HAL's
                own access code does not set it.
                Evidence: `modules/hal/espressif/components/esp_hal_regi2c/esp32c6/regi2c_impl.c:112-180`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>
                Busy; polled after each command word.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WRITE</name>
              <description>
                Set for a write, clear for a read.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>
                Data to write; after a read, the result.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>REG_ADDR</name>
              <description>
                Register number in the block.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BLOCK</name>
              <description>
                Analog block id.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ANA_CONF1</name>
          <description>
            Read mask
            Other bits: documented
            Evidence: `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/include/modem/i2c_ana_mst_reg.h:105-150`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ANA_CONF1</name>
              <description>
                The read mask.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[23:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ANA_CONF2</name>
          <description>
            Master select
            Other bits: documented
            Evidence: `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/include/modem/i2c_ana_mst_reg.h:105-150`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ANA_CONF2</name>
              <description>
                The master select. The ROM's `get_i2c_hostid()` keeps the register's bits
                outside 17:4 and ORs in 0x1fe00.
                Evidence: *decompile* C6 ROM 0x40003d4a
              </description>
              <bitRange>[23:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>ULP</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x61 (ULP)
        Registers 0 to 10 at 2412 MHz: `1f 02 08 49 53 40 51 2f 33 59 20` (bench
        2026-10-09). The Init rows below come from this dump.
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6100</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>11</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R4</name>
          <description>
            HAL `I2C_ULP_OCODE`, function undocumented
            Init: 0x53
          </description>
          <addressOffset>4</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OCODE</name>
              <description>
                HAL name `I2C_ULP_OCODE`; the function is undocumented. Reads 0x4f instead
                of 0x53 after the native 802.15.4 radio ran in the previous firmware (see
                "Stale modem state").
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09;
                `modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32c6/include/soc/regi2c_lp_bias.h:45`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R9</name>
          <description>
            Unknown
            Init: 0x59
            Reads 0x5a in the same stale state as ULP_OCODE (see "Stale modem
            state").
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
          </description>
          <addressOffset>9</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>RFPLL</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x62 (RF PLL)
        The RF PLL's VCO and its calibration (the S3 has the same block). The library reads
        RFPLL_VCO_WINDOW after every tune and drives the LO 5/6 divider through
        RFPLL_LO_DIV. The capacitor code is `0x62:5 | 0x62:7[2] &lt;&lt; 8` (9 bits; the
        vendor clamps to 0x1ff); a higher code means a lower VCO frequency.

        Some field meanings come from the S3 vendor code, as the fields and their evidence
        say.

        Upstream esp-sdr does a manual 512-code search on the ESP32-S2 through the same
        fields (RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE, RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE_B8, RFPLL_CODE_SRC, `0x62:0[7]`,
        and RFPLL_VCO_WINDOW as the window indication); it does not apply it to the S3 or
        the C6 (*upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32s2/pll.h:8-36`).

        Registers 0 to 18 at 2412 MHz:
        `68 bb 8d 61 28 bb 0d 03 80 f0 a0 44 00 00 14 74 07 ff 60` (bench 2026-10-09).
        The Init rows below come from this dump.
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6200</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>19</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R0</name>
          <description>
            VCO calibration start
            Init: 0x68
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 ROM `restart_cal` (0x4000587e), S3 `phy_rfpll.o`;
            *measured* C6 dump
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R0_B6</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; toggled around the calibration start (see RFPLL_CAL_START).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CAL_START</name>
              <description>
                `restart_cal()` starts the VCO calibration with a rising edge on this bit
                while RFPLL_R0_B6 is toggled: the C6 clears RFPLL_R0_B6, pulses this bit and
                sets RFPLL_R0_B6; the S3 sets it first and clears it after.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R1</name>
          <description>
            Manual capacitor code, bits 7:0
            Init: 0xbb, the same as RFPLL_R5 (see also "Stale modem state")
          </description>
          <addressOffset>1</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MANUAL_CODE</name>
              <description>
                Bits 7:0 of a manual capacitor code. The meaning comes from the S3 vendor
                code, where `write_pll_cap()` writes the code here. After a tune it holds the
                low byte of the code in use (on the C6 it reads as RFPLL_R5).
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`; *measured* C6 dump
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R2</name>
          <description>
            Manual code bit 8 and a 4-bit code override
            Init: 0x8d, read with RFPLL_R6 = 0x0d
            The 4-bit code override: the S3's `get_rf_freq_init()` clears RFPLL_CODE4_OVR,
            calibrates at 2437 MHz, copies RFPLL_CODE4_CAL into RFPLL_CODE4_VAL and sets
            RFPLL_CODE4_OVR. What the 4-bit code controls is unknown.
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_hw_freq.o` (override sequence); *measured* C6 dump
          </description>
          <addressOffset>2</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CODE4_OVR</name>
              <description>
                Cleared before and set after the vendor copies the calibrated 4-bit code
                into RFPLL_CODE4_VAL (see below).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MANUAL_CODE_B8</name>
              <description>
                Bit 8 of the manual capacitor code, written with RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE. The
                meaning comes from the S3 vendor code.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CODE4_VAL</name>
              <description>
                The 4-bit code of the override, copied from RFPLL_CODE4_CAL (see below).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R4</name>
          <description>
            Unknown
            Init: 0x28 (40) at every PLL frequency read, 2280 to 2438 MHz
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
          </description>
          <addressOffset>4</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R5</name>
          <description>
            Capacitor code readback, bits 7:0
            Init: 0xbb
          </description>
          <addressOffset>5</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CAP_CODE</name>
              <description>
                Bits 7:0 of the capacitor code; bit 8 is RFPLL_CAP_CODE_B8. Values: see
                "Measured PLL behaviour".
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`; *measured*
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R6</name>
          <description>
            Calibrated 4-bit code
            Init: 0x0d
          </description>
          <addressOffset>6</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CODE4_CAL</name>
              <description>
                The calibrated 4-bit code. The vendor copies it into RFPLL_CODE4_VAL (see
                RFPLL_R2).
                Evidence: *decompile* as RFPLL_R2
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R7</name>
          <description>
            Capacitor code bit 8 and calibration done
            Init: 0x03
          </description>
          <addressOffset>7</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CAP_CODE_B8</name>
              <description>
                Bit 8 of the capacitor code readback (see RFPLL_CAP_CODE).
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`; *measured*
              </description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CAL_DONE</name>
              <description>
                Calibration done; 1 after every tune. The S3's `wait_rfpll_cal_end()` polls
                it every 20 us, 100 times, then prints
                `error: pll_cal exceeds 2ms!!!`.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`; *measured*
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R11</name>
          <description>
            Capacitor code source
            Init: 0x44
          </description>
          <addressOffset>11</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CODE_SRC</name>
              <description>
                Cleared before each software calibration (`set_rfpll_freq()`), set by
                `rfpll_cap_init_cal()` before it tries manual codes. Inferred: 0 = code from
                the calibration engine, 1 = code from RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE and
                RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE_B8.
                Evidence: *decompile* C6 ROM `set_rfpll_freq` and `rfpll_cap_init_cal`,
                S3 `phy_rfpll.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R12</name>
          <description>
            VCO window comparator and two unknown status bits
            Init: 0x00
          </description>
          <addressOffset>12</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>VCO_WINDOW</name>
              <description>
                After calibration: 0 = the VCO inside its tuning window; 1 = target below
                the window (code pinned at its maximum); 2 = target above it (code 0). Live
                for the current code: the S3 vendor's `rfpll_cap_correct()` moves the code
                in steps of 2, up while it reads 1 and down while it reads 2.
                `esp_sdr_set_freq()` and `esp_sdr_set_freq_offset()` return -ERANGE when it
                is not 0. Window (PLL): 2140 to 2884 MHz.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09; *working*
                `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:78-97, 194-196, 213-215`; *decompile* S3
                `phy_rfpll.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R12_B1</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; set from about 2700 MHz (PLL) up, on both chips.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R12_B0</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; set below about 2412 MHz.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
              </description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R14</name>
          <description>
            Charge pump calibration done and result
            Init: 0x14
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 `libphy.a` `phy_rfpll.o` (`rfpll_chgp_cal`)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>14</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CHGP_CAL_DONE</name>
              <description>
                Charge pump calibration done (see RFPLL_R15).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CHGP_CAL_RESULT</name>
              <description>
                Charge pump calibration result (see RFPLL_R15).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R15</name>
          <description>
            Charge pump calibration
            Init: 0x74
            `rfpll_chgp_cal()` clears RFPLL_R15_B6, pulses RFPLL_R15_B5, polls
            RFPLL_CHGP_CAL_DONE (20 us, 100 tries), reads r = RFPLL_CHGP_CAL_RESULT, sets
            RFPLL_R15_B6 and writes RFPLL_CHGP_CODE = min(31, 7r / 6 + 9). The C6 read
            RFPLL_R14 = 0x14 and RFPLL_R15 = 0x74 at 2412 MHz, which this formula does not
            produce, so the reading is incomplete or RFPLL_R14 changes later.
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 `libphy.a` `phy_rfpll.o` (`rfpll_chgp_cal`);
            *measured* C6 dump
          </description>
          <addressOffset>15</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R15_B6</name>
              <description>
                Cleared before the charge pump calibration and set after it (see below).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R15_B5</name>
              <description>
                Pulsed before RFPLL_CHGP_CAL_DONE is polled (see below).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CHGP_CODE</name>
              <description>
                Written from the calibration result, min(31, 7r / 6 + 9) (see below).
              </description>
              <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R16</name>
          <description>
            LO 5/6 divider select
            Init: 0x07
            - Change only the bit (read-modify-write). Clear it before the vendor tuning
              call, which calibrates in normal mode; set it after the receive front-end
              setup.
            - The library uses the divider for every frequency below 2210 MHz (PLL at 1.2
              times the LO), upstream only from 1842 MHz.
            - A scan of the C6 blob objects found no access to `0x62:16`.
            - Upstream qualifies the selector on the ESP32, S2, S3, C2, C3 and C6 and warns
              that the C5, C61, H2 and S31 lay out their analog blocks differently.
            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:106-108`,
            `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:92-95` (when to clear and set)
            Evidence: `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:65-76`,
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/rx_lo.h:15-19` (divider band)
            Evidence: *decompile*, objdump (no access in the C6 blobs)
            Evidence: `modules/lib/esp-sdr/docs/rx-controls.md:102-107` (qualified chips)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>16</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LO_DIV</name>
              <description>
                0: the LO is the PLL. 1: LO = PLL / 1.2 (5/6 conversion). One selector feeds
                both the receive and the transmit mixer. Mask 0x08; the S3 has the same
                selector in block 0x65, which the C6 does not have.
                Evidence: *upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/rx_lo.h:47-62`,
                external-tone tests `modules/lib/esp-sdr/docs/rx-controls.md:76-100`;
                *measured* on air for receive and transmit (bench 2026-10-09)
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R17</name>
          <description>
            Unknown
            Init: 0xff at 2412 MHz; 0x7e at 2413 MHz
            Evidence: *measured* C6 dump
          </description>
          <addressOffset>17</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R18</name>
          <description>
            Unknown
            Init: 0x60
            Evidence: *measured* C6 dump
          </description>
          <addressOffset>18</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>RFPLL_SDM</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x63 (RF PLL fractional-N divider)
        Registers 0 and 3 to 6:

        `f_PLL = 1.5 * f_xtal * (32 + r3 + (r4 * 2^11 + r5 * 2^3 + r6[2:0]) / 2^19)`

        That is 60 MHz per integer step with the 40 MHz crystal. The vendor computes a
        word `W = r3 &lt;&lt; 19 | r4 &lt;&lt; 11 | r5 &lt;&lt; 3 | r6` with
        `f_PLL = 3 * f_xtal * (16 + W / 2^20)`; W bit 27 is computed but not written.
        `write_rfpll_sdm()` clears RFPLL_SDM_R0_B3 (`0x63:0[3]`), writes registers 3 to 6
        and sets the bit again.

        | PLL MHz | reg 3 | reg 4 | reg 5 | reg 6 | Note |
        |---|---|---|---|---|---|
        | 2412 | 0x08 | 0x33 | 0x33 | 0x01 | |
        | 2413 | 0x08 | 0x37 | 0x77 | 0x03 | |
        | 2417 | 0x08 | 0x48 | 0x88 | 0x04 | |
        | 2437 | 0x08 | 0x9d | 0xdd | 0x06 | |
        | 2438 | 0x08 | 0xa2 | 0x22 | 0x01 | |
        | 2280 | 0x06 | 0x00 | 0x00 | 0x00 | LO 1900 MHz through the divider |
        | 2404 | 0x08 | 0x11 | 0x11 | 0x00 | asked for 2412 MHz in the stale modem state |

        Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09; *decompile* C6 ROM `write_rfpll_sdm`
        (0x400058ee), C6 `libphy.a` `phy_rfpll.o` (`ram_rfpll_set_freq`).

        The bench reading `60 MHz * (N + F / 2^20)` with N = 32 + reg 3 and
        `F = r4 &lt;&lt; 12 | r5 &lt;&lt; 4 | r6` is the same formula except that the vendor
        code weights reg 6 twice as much (it holds W bits 2:0), a difference below
        0.2 kHz. The bench reading also took reg 3 as the low 4 bits of N; the vendor code
        puts 8 bits there (N - 32), which agrees for every value seen.

        The crystal selector of the vendor calls picks the divisor, as on the S3: 1 =
        26 MHz, 2 = 32 MHz, anything else = 40 MHz (*decompile* C6 `ram_rfpll_set_freq`).

        The Init rows below are the values at 2412 MHz.
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6300</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>7</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R0</name>
          <description>
            Bit 3 cleared and set around a divider write; other bits unknown
            Init: 0x5b
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R0_B3</name>
              <description>
                `write_rfpll_sdm()` clears it, writes RFPLL_SDM_R3 to RFPLL_SDM_R6 and sets
                it again.
                Evidence: *decompile* C6 ROM `write_rfpll_sdm` (0x400058ee)
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R1</name>
          <description>
            Unknown
            Init: 0xab
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
          </description>
          <addressOffset>1</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R3</name>
          <description>
            Divider integer part
            Init: 0x08
          </description>
          <addressOffset>3</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>INTEGER</name>
              <description>
                r3 in the formula: N - 32. The vendor code puts 8 bits here.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R4</name>
          <description>
            Divider fraction, high byte
            Init: 0x33
          </description>
          <addressOffset>4</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FRAC_H</name>
              <description>
                r4 in the formula.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R5</name>
          <description>
            Divider fraction, middle byte
            Init: 0x33
          </description>
          <addressOffset>5</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FRAC_M</name>
              <description>
                r5 in the formula.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R6</name>
          <description>
            Divider fraction, low 3 bits
            Init: 0x01
          </description>
          <addressOffset>6</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FRAC_L</name>
              <description>
                r6[2:0] in the formula: W bits 2:0.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[2:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BBPLL</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x66 (BBPLL)
        Documented by the HAL; esp_sdr does not touch the block. Upstream looked for other
        ADC rates (on the S3 through this block); for the C6 it reports that no reliable
        lower-rate path was found (*upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/docs/rx-controls.md:59-61`).

        Registers 0 to 10 at 2412 MHz: `18 25 50 08 6b 80 73 00 c2 96 03` (bench
        2026-10-09).
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6600</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>11</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BBTOP</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x67 (BBTOP, analog baseband)
        The C6 libphy groups the filter codes as the S3 does (`filter_dcap_set()`:
        registers 4, 5, 12, 13 from one PHY parameter, 6, 7, 14, 15 from the next), so the
        transmit pairs are probably at the same place as on the S3 (*decompile* C6
        `libphy.a` `phy_i2c.o`). `esp_sdr_tx_set_lpf()` returns -ENOTSUP on the C6.
        `esp_sdr_bbtop_read()` and `esp_sdr_bbtop_write()` give unchecked access
        (`lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:648-667`).
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6700</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>57</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>4,5</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Receive low-pass capacitor code, I (BBTOP_R4) and Q (BBTOP_R5)
            Bits 7:6 are kept.
          </description>
          <addressOffset>4</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RX_LPF</name>
              <description>
                Values 0 to 63; larger is narrower, 54 to 12 MHz on the C6. 0 is the widest
                setting, not a bypass.
                Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:126-148`; *upstream*
                curves `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/rx_bandwidth.h:79-84`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>12,13</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Probably the transmit low-pass pair A, as on the S3
            `filter_dcap_set()` writes them from the same PHY parameter as BBTOP_R4 and
            BBTOP_R5 (see the introduction).
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 `libphy.a` `phy_i2c.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>12</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>14,15</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Probably the transmit low-pass pair B, as on the S3
            `filter_dcap_set()` writes them from the same PHY parameter as registers 6 and 7
            (see the introduction).
            Evidence: *decompile* C6 `libphy.a` `phy_i2c.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>14</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BIAS</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x6a (bias)
        The HAL names the block (`I2C_BIAS`). Registers 0 to 3 at 2412 MHz read
        `cf 7c 1a 07` (bench 2026-10-09).
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6a00</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
    </peripheral>
  </peripherals>
</device>
